- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit value?
- What are the risks of using this in performance-critical hot paths compared to builtin intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of bitwise compression patterns and correct use of masks. Avoids loops and relies on proven parallel bit extraction tactics.

## Key Concepts
- compact1by1 technique
- masks and shifts for bit packing
- 64→32-bit narrowing without branches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Signature example (conceptual):
uint32_t packEvenBits64(uint64_t x) {
  x &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x | (x>>1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x | (x>>2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = (x | (x>>4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  x = (x | (x>>8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  return (uint32_t)(x | (x>>16));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to implement a classic bit-twiddling technique (popcount) on 128-bit integers without branching. This skill is useful in hash tables, connectivity algorithms, and low-latency code.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting (divide-and-conquer approach)
- Splitting a 128-bit value into two 64-bit chunks
- Branchless arithmetic using bitwise masks

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount64(uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
    return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-word bit ops and branchless parity reduction. It exercises combining two 64-bit halves and a compact reduction, scalable to wider architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Parity via XOR folding
- 128-bit composition hi^lo
- Branchless reductions

## Code Example

```c
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return x & 1;
}
```

